gomodel:6ab067da00007187
        a                      owl:Ontology ;
        rdfs:comment           "2026-10-13: OXM hormone activity BP changed from GO:0016486 'peptide hormone processing' (the PCSK1 process that produces OXM, not one OXM acts in) to GO:0007189 'adenylate cyclase-activating G protein-coupled receptor signaling pathway', per the receptor-ligand guideline (ligand part_of the signaling it triggers); GO has no GLP1R- or GCGR-specific pathway term. Evidence IDA PMID:17395766 (OXM full agonist for GLP1R-mediated cAMP accumulation); receptor species not stated in the abstract, full text not checked. Matches the companion model gomodel:6ab067da00005905." , "Seam (steps 5->7): CREB1 has_input the GCG gene and is 'causally upstream of, positive effect' of PCSK1 endopeptidase activity. CREB1 supplies PCSK1's substrate (proglucagon) via transcription+translation; it does NOT regulate the convertase itself, so a regulation relation would misstate the mechanism. GO-CAM has no translation MF, so no separate synthesis node was created." , "Ligand: bile acid anion (CHEBI:36235, pH-7.3 conjugate-base form; NOT CHEBI:3098 'bile acid', which is the neutral acid class) linked to GPBAR1 by 'is small molecule activator of' (RO:0012005), per the small-molecule-activated receptor pattern. IDA PMID:12524422 (bile acids raise cAMP in CHO cells expressing TGR5). The generic class was chosen over lithocholate (CHEBI:29744, most potent TGR5 agonist) or chenodeoxycholate (CHEBI:36234, raises plasma OXM in PMID:33459181) because the deorphanization paper does not single out one species and PMID:33459181 does not show CDCA acting through GPBAR1." , "Evidence notes. Human experimental data DO exist for this chain, contrary to an all-ISS assumption: PMID:36343264 (PNAS 2022) genetically engineered human enteroendocrine organoids, PCSK1 knockout, LC-MS/MS -> IMP for the PCSK1 node; GPBAR1 MF is human IDA (PMID:12524422). Rodent anchors used as ISS: PMID:15085339 (dominant-negative PKA/CREB block proglucagon transcription, mouse STC-1), PMID:17698597 (PC1/3 switch -> OXM, mouse). PMID:9528943 (PC1 overexpression raises OXM) is a HAMSTER line (InR1-G9) with no MOD id, so it is cited here only as support, not as an ISS with/from source." , "Source-cell biosynthetic chain for oxyntomodulin: GPBAR1 -> Gs -> ADCY6 -> cAMP -> PKA -> CREB1 -> (GCG transcription + translation, not represented) -> PCSK1 -> OXM. Companion to gomodel:6ab067da00005905 (OXM secretion/action via GLP1R), which shares the PCSK1->OXM step." , "PCSK1 node deviates from the source spec: no 'has output' oxyntomodulin. GO-CAM best practice says not to capture has_output when the has_input is a gene product; the handoff is a causal edge to the OXM hormone activity unit instead." , "Caveats. (1) ADCY6 is a placeholder: the adenylate cyclase isoform coupled in the L cell is not established; the source spec said 'ADCY family'. (2) GPBAR1->Gs and Gs->ADCY6 are curator inferences (IC), not assays in L cells. (3) PKA is placed in the nucleus (CREB Ser133 phosphorylation), not the cytosol as in the source spec. (4) CPE C-terminal trim was deliberately NOT modelled: no evidence found that CPE acts on oxyntomodulin specifically. (5) The Wnt/TCF7L2-beta-catenin arm (PMID:15525634) is an alternative, L-cell-selective entry into GCG transcription and is not modelled here. (6) This chain is shared by all proglucagon-derived peptides; it is OXM-specific only at the PCSK1 output." , "Built by claude4go with curator 2026-10-12. Source spec checked: all 8 accessions correct; GO:0019933 'cAMP-mediated signaling' is OBSOLETE (replaced here by GO:0007189 / GO:0141156); GO:0004930 -> GO:0038182 and GO:0003700 -> GO:0001228 for specificity. Abstract-level reading only, no full texts." ;
